# Project Rules

## Project Shape
- This project uses the four-role SwarmForge flow: specifier, coder,
  refactorer, and architect.
- It is a pnpm workspace for a strict TypeScript CLI and independently runnable
  test applications, all requiring Node 22 or newer.
- The Auto-QA CLI remains the root package unless a dedicated migration moves
  it; test applications live under `apps/*`.
- Use pnpm through FNM with Node 22.
- Effect owns services, resources, and typed errors. Commander parses arguments
  only. Playwright is the browser implementation and Vitest is the test runner.
- Babashka is SwarmForge and Acceptance Pipeline Specification infrastructure;
  it is not the application language.

## Required Project Guidance
- Read `AGENTS.md`, `CONTEXT.md`, `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md`,
  `docs/system-overview.md`, `docs/implementation-slices.md`,
  `docs/testing-strategy.md`, and relevant ADRs before changing behavior.
- Treat the accepted ADRs as binding unless the user explicitly reopens one.
- Work through implementation slices in order and keep each handoff to one
  coherent vertical tracer bullet.
- Never hardcode a Base URL. The Target Application is externally owned.

## Verification
- Run focused tests through `fnm exec --using 22 pnpm vitest run <file>`.
- Run the fast suite with `fnm exec --using 22 pnpm test`.
- Run type checking with `fnm exec --using 22 pnpm typecheck`.
- Run lint with `fnm exec --using 22 pnpm lint`.
- Keep generated Gherkin acceptance tests, real-browser tests, crash tests, and
  mutation tests behind separate stable scripts.

## Local Configuration
- Preserve project-local SwarmForge configuration under `swarmforge/`.
- Keep runtime state under `.swarmforge/`, role worktrees under `.worktrees/`,
  and copied shared scripts under ignored `swarmforge/scripts/`.

## Handoffs
- Keep handoffs terse and explicit. Report the committed state and request the
  recipient's role-specific work; do not include verification logs or process
  narrative.

## Ownership
- Do not change another role's prompt or workflow ownership without explicit
  user direction.
